/*
    mmbasic_repl_v86.tc
    ------------------
    Interactive Tiny-C MMBASIC-style REPL for the RP2350/Pico VM.

    This replaces the Phase 1 SD-output runner with a real framebuffer text
    console, keyboard line entry, and in-memory numbered BASIC program storage.

    Console:
      - NARM MODE 5: 320x240 RGB332, 53 columns x 30 rows (startup).
      - NARM MODE 3: 640x480 4-bit palette, 106 columns x 60 rows.
      - Both modes use the 5x7 font and the existing RGB332 colour arguments.
      - MODE clears the screen; MM.HRES/MM.VRES/MM.BPP/MM.MODE query it.
      - RGB(r,g,b) packs 8-bit components to RGB332. MODE 3 maps colours to
        the nearest entry in the firmware's fixed 16-colour palette.
      - Uses fast page-clear overflow instead of full-screen redraw scrolling.
      - BASIC output goes directly to the screen.

    Keyboard:
      - Common PS/2 Set-2 non-extended scancodes.
      - Enter = 0x5A, Backspace = 0x66.
      - Uses PC-style keydown/key-up behavior: one character on fresh keydown,
        no extra input until key-up, unless the key is held for 750 ms; then it
        repeats periodically.
      - Left/right arrows move the edit cursor.
      - Insertion and Backspace work at the cursor position.
      - The edit line is horizontally viewported and redraws only changed cells,
        so long input lines stay responsive.
      - Left/right shift are polled for shifted symbols.
      - Esc is not used; the OS reserves it.

    REPL commands:
      RUN       run the stored program
      LIST      list the stored program
      NEW       clear program and variables
      LOAD      load BASIC.TXT from SD into memory
      LOAD "path" load a BASIC file from a path/directory
      SAVE      save current program to BASIC.TXT
      SAVE "path" save current program to a path/directory
      MEM       show interpreter memory/capacity still available
      CLS       clear screen
      HELP      show short help
      BYE       halt

    Program editing:
      10 PRINT "HELLO"     stores/replaces line 10
      10                   deletes line 10

    BASIC subset inherited from phase 1:
      PRINT, INPUT, LET, NAME=expr, IF expr rel expr THEN line, GOTO,
      FOR/NEXT, REM, END/STOP.
      Drawing statements: GCLS, PIXEL/PSET, LINE, RECT, FILLRECT, TRI, FILLTRI, GTEXT, GBUTTON, PRESENT.
      Subroutines: GOSUB line and RETURN.
      GPIO statements: PINMODE/GPIOMODE, GPIOSET/PINSET/DIGITALWRITE.
      Expression functions: MILLIS(), RND(), RND(n), RAND(), RAND(n), RANDOM(), RANDOM(n), RAND32().
      Timing commands: PAUSE ms, DELAY ms.
      Console drawing uses native mm_fill_rect() and mm_glyph()/putGlyph() VM opcodes.
      Integer variables with MMBasic-style names up to 32 chars. Old A..Z variables still work.
      String variables ending in $ are supported: NAME$, A$, PLAYER.NAME$.
      DIM arrays: A(10), GRID(9,9), NAME$(20), indexed with expressions.
      v17 fixes DIM name preservation when dimension limits use variables, e.g. DIM A(LEN).
      v18 fixes recursive expression parsing, so array references inside larger expressions work.
      v20 adds MMBasic-style labels and multiple statements per line with :.
      v21 adds multiline IF/ELSE/ENDIF and DO/LOOP block control flow.
      v22 adds WHILE/WEND and SELECT CASE/CASE/CASE ELSE/END SELECT.
      v23 adds common string functions and float numeric expressions/variables.
      v24 adds PAUSE/DELAY.
      v25 adds DATA/READ/RESTORE and first-pass SUB/FUNCTION/LOCAL.
      v27 changes OPEN file I/O to chunked SD-offset reads/writes so files are no longer limited by the old per-handle buffer.
      v28 adds a simple MMBasic programming environment mode with colors,
          file-oriented commands, and a switch back to the classic REPL.
      v29 renames the environment to EDITOR, supports unnumbered stored program lines,
          adds simple syntax-colored LIST/EDIT output, and makes TAB insert indentation.
      v30 replaces the command-line editor with a fullscreen source editor with cursor
          navigation, in-place editing, keyword/variable/literal/comment coloring,
          and editor shortcuts.
      v31 refines the editor UI colors/layout, removes F1/F2 shortcuts, and adds
          persistent editor color configuration in EDITOR.CFG.
      v32 adjusts editor spacing so the divider lines do not touch text and the
          source area starts below the upper divider.
      v34 refines editor pixel offsets: source text 4 px up/1 px right, footer
          divider 1 px up, and classic REPL console 1 px down/right.
      v37 targets the register-based KBRX1/key() compiler: KEYDOWN(scancode) now calls key(scancode, ...) directly.
      v41 drastically speeds up editor F5/RUN startup by using lazy runtime clears
          instead of wiping every large string/DATA/array buffer before execution.
      v56 clamps lazy-clear counters on boot so stale VM memory cannot hang before first draw.
      v42 adds a bytecode-only RUN path. Source text is tokenized before RUN;
          the program runtime no longer dispatches statements by reading ASCII words.
      v45 keeps the bytecode runtime but must be compiled with a larger stack reserve
          because the bytecode evaluator has recursive expression/function paths. Using
          only --stack-words 4096 can corrupt VM return addresses and make the VM
          jump into BASIC token data, commonly showing illegal VM opcode 128.
      v76 makes built-in function names context-sensitive when tokenizing: names like
          LEN are only emitted as function tokens when followed by '(', so PI.BAS can
          use LEN as a normal numeric variable.  It also improves bytecode error line
          reporting by mapping bcPc back to bcLineStart/bcLineEnd.
      v77 fixes parenthesized numeric subexpressions in the bytecode evaluator.
          A closing ')' now ends the current expression only when it belongs to the caller;
          otherwise it closes an internal expression group like X % (2 * I - 1).
      v78 restores integer division for int/int arithmetic. Float division still
          happens when at least one operand is already float.
      v79 keeps FOR variables integer when start/end/step are integer.
      v80 makes the v79 FOR fix safer on real hardware: it removes the added
          bcForIsFloat global array and avoids extra locals in bc_exec_statement.
          The FOR float flag is packed into bcForVar[] instead.
      v81 fixes bytecode RUN state leakage: normal BASIC END/STOP now stops only
          bcRunning and no longer leaves the interpreter-wide running flag at 0.
          bc_run_program() also saves/restores the caller running state, so the
          fullscreen editor/source restore path does not run after BASIC END with
          stale "runtime stopped" state.
      v82 raises program/source capacity for larger BASIC programs and increases
          string capacity from 95 usable chars to 255 usable chars.
      v83 stores editable BASIC source text in packed char[] storage instead of
          one int32 VM word per source character.  Bytecode still uses int[]
          because tokens/operands are word-sized, but source, strings, DATA text,
          save/load buffers, and bytecode string literals are packed by vmcc.
      v84 stores the unified bytecode stream in packed uint8[] storage.  Each
          old word-sized token/operand is encoded as unsigned LEB128 bytes, so
          small opcodes and punctuation take 1-2 bytes while large integer
          operands automatically spread across more bytes.
      v85 removes the leftover 524288-byte prog[] staging arena.  LOAD/SAVE now
          stream through a 256-byte packed char buffer, while prog[] is only the
          current LINE_MAX-sized parser line.  Several metadata/flag arrays were
          changed to uint8/uint16 so vmcc packs them instead of wasting int32 words.
      v86 removes independent source-line, scalar-variable, and DIM-array
          storage ceilings.  Editable source slots grow upward in one packed
          BASIC memory pool.  Scalar records and numeric/string DIM payloads
          grow downward in the same pool.  Programs can use more lines or more
          variables whenever the other side leaves room, matching the shared
          memory behavior of original MMBasic more closely.
      v87 updates SD file calls for the new vmcc file API, uses sdMount()
          before SD load/save/file operations, switches chunk transfers to
          byte-counted sdFileReadBytes()/sdFileWriteBytes(), implements NAME
          by copy/delete, and adds Shift+arrow text selection in the editor.
*/

/* The BASIC numeric engine uses binary32. Floating constants below carry
   explicit f suffixes for current NARMCC C double-literal semantics. */

